{"code":0,"data":{"title":"A Quick Introduction to the MongoDB Cloud Platform","content":"# A Quick Introduction to the MongoDB Cloud Platform\n\n# What Is MongoDB Cloud[ #](#what-is-mongodb-cloud)\n\nAt the core of MongoDB Cloud is [MongoDB Atlas](https://www.mongodb.com/zh-cn/cloud/atlas), a fully managed cloud database for modern applications. Atlas is the best way to run MongoDB, the leading modern database. [MongoDB](https://www.mongodb.com/zh-cn/what-is-mongodb)'s document model is a faster way to innovate, bringing flexibility and ease of use to the database.\n\nThat is the official description. Next, let's look at the pricing:\n\n![Pricing](/post/mongodbcloudcluster/5e3ecd48dd31.png)\n\nAs you can see, there is a free shared tier. It runs as a sandbox environment and already includes basic data storage. If you need more storage, network isolation, and so on, consider the other two tiers.\n\n# Creating a DB Cluster[ #](#creating-a-db-cluster)\n\n## Log In[ #](#log-in)\n\nFirst, log in to your MongoDB Cloud account. If you don't have one, create an account, log in, and go to the Dashboard:\n\n![Dashboard](/post/mongodbcloudcluster/55e80b53d456.png)\n\n> Note: a cluster has already been created in the screenshot above.\n\n## Create a Cluster[ #](#create-a-cluster)\n\nFrom the Dashboard you can create a cluster and configure it, including the data region, the cloud provider, and other settings. ![Create cluster](/post/mongodbcloudcluster/70d232773614.png)\n\nPick a low-latency data region based on your service needs:\n\n![Region](/post/mongodbcloudcluster/36823cf56cee.png)\n\nAnd the cloud provider:\n\n![Provider](/post/mongodbcloudcluster/0d5e3adf8ab3.png)\n\nYou can also configure some additional cluster settings (for example, the database version to use):\n\n![Settings](/post/mongodbcloudcluster/c4322124a40f.png)\n\n## Configure the Cluster[ #](#configure-the-cluster)\n\nThe configuration is fairly simple and has two main parts: under Data, trigger configuration; under Security, database user configuration and network access configuration.\n\nDatabase user configuration adds users who can access the database, including setting a username and password; certificate-based authentication is also an option:\n\n![Add user](/post/mongodbcloudcluster/04695e885b07.png)\n\nAfter creating a user, we still cannot log in to the database: we also need to add our client's IP address to the allowlist:\n\n![IP allowlist](/post/mongodbcloudcluster/e67ad7e2dd3d.png)\n\n> Note:\n>\n> Don't forget to add your IP to the allowlist, or connections will fail.\n\n# Connecting to and Using the DB Cluster[ #](#connecting-to-and-using-the-db-cluster)\n\nOn the client, use `mongo` to connect to the cloud cluster we just created:\n\n![Connect to cloud](/post/mongodbcloudcluster/6fde4896f761.png)\n\nOnce connected, you can write some data into it:\n\n![Collection](/post/mongodbcloudcluster/35c3a075cc43.png)\n","locale":"en","slug":"mongodbcloudcluster"}}